%I A400182 #15 Oct 08 2026 14:18:02
%S A400182 1,1,3,2,4,3,2,15,14,5,13,4,12,3,10,11,2,9,10,5,9,18,9,4,9,17,3,8,18,
%T A400182 9,16,16,7,2,17,9,15,15,6,6,23,16,9,14,14,14,5,5,22,15,22,9,13,13,13,
%U A400182 4,4,23,21,14,21,9,21,12,7,12,3,7,22,3,20,13,20,9,29,20
%N A400182 Number of steps starting from the n-th positive integer not divisible by 5 until the first repeated value under the reduced residue-dependent 6k +- {1,2} map, or -1 if no value ever repeats.
%C A400182 a(n) is the number of iterations of T, starting from A047201(n), until a value occurs that has already appeared earlier in the same trajectory, where
%C A400182 T(k) = (6*k-1)/5^v_5(6*k-1) if k == 1 (mod 5),
%C A400182 T(k) = (6*k-2)/5^v_5(6*k-2) if k == 2 (mod 5),
%C A400182 T(k) = (6*k+2)/5^v_5(6*k+2) if k == 3 (mod 5),
%C A400182 T(k) = (6*k+1)/5^v_5(6*k+1) if k == 4 (mod 5).
%C A400182 Here A047201(n) = floor((5*n-1)/4) is the n-th positive integer not divisible by 5, and v_5(m) = A112765(m).
%C A400182 The known cycles are the fixed points 1 and 2 and the nine-cycle
%C A400182 22 -> 26 -> 31 -> 37 -> 44 -> 53 -> 64 -> 77 -> 92 -> 22.
%C A400182 If a trajectory has preperiod length mu and eventual cycle length lambda, then a(n) = mu + lambda.
%C A400182 Thus a starting value already on one of the fixed points has value 1, while a starting value already on the nine-cycle has value 9.
%C A400182 The linked computation exhaustively verified that every positive starting value k <= 10^11 with 5 not dividing k enters one of the three displayed cycles.
%C A400182 It is not known whether every positive integer, after removal of initial factors of 5, eventually enters a cycle; additional cycles or divergent trajectories have not been excluded globally.
%H A400182 Farhad Banazadeh, <a href="https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.22786203">The Reduced 6k +- {1, 2}/5 Collatz-Type Domain with Three Observed Attractors up to 10^11</a>, Zenodo, 2026.
%F A400182 Let b(n) = A047201(n) = floor((5*n-1)/4).
%F A400182 a(n) = min { j >= 1 : T^j(b(n)) = T^i(b(n)) for some 0 <= i < j }, if such j exists; otherwise a(n) = -1.
%F A400182 Equivalently, if the trajectory of b(n) has preperiod length mu and eventual period lambda, then a(n) = mu + lambda.
%e A400182 For n = 1, A047201(1) = 1 and the orbit begins 1, 1.
%e A400182 The first repeated value occurs after 1 iteration, so a(1) = 1.
%e A400182 For n = 3, A047201(3) = 3 and the orbit is 3, 4, 1, 1.
%e A400182 The first repeated value occurs after 3 iterations, so a(3) = 3.
%e A400182 For n = 20, A047201(20) = 24 and the orbit is 24, 29, 7, 8, 2, 2.
%e A400182 The first repeated value occurs after 5 iterations, so a(20) = 5.
%e A400182 For n = 29, A047201(29) = 36 and the orbit is 36, 43, 52, 62, 74, 89, 107, 128, 154, 37, 44, 53, 64, 77, 92, 22, 26, 31, 37.
%e A400182 The first repeated value is 37, occurring after 18 iterations, so a(29) = 18.
%o A400182 (Python)
%o A400182 def T(k):
%o A400182     y = 6*k + {1:-1, 2:-2, 3:2, 4:1}[k % 5]
%o A400182     while y % 5 == 0:
%o A400182         y //= 5
%o A400182     return y
%o A400182 def a(n):
%o A400182     k, seen = (5*n - 1)//4, set()
%o A400182     while k not in seen:
%o A400182         seen.add(k)
%o A400182         k = T(k)
%o A400182     return len(seen)
%Y A400182 Cf. A006666, A047201, A112765, A330920, A400174.
%K A400182 nonn,new
%O A400182 1,3
%A A400182 _Farhad Banazadeh_, Sep 20 2026
